      "text": "To determine the smallest possible degree of a nonzero polynomial with rational coefficients that has the numbers \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, \\; 2+\\sqrt{3}, \\; 3+\\sqrt{4},\\; \\dots, \\;1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) as roots, we need to understand the properties of polynomial roots with rational coefficients. Specifically, if a polynomial with rational coefficients has a root that is an irrational number of the form \\(a + \\sqrt{b}\\), then its conjugate \\(a - \\sqrt{b}\\) must also be a root of the polynomial.\n\nGiven the roots \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, \\; 2+\\sqrt{3}, \\; 3+\\sqrt{4},\\; \\dots, \\;1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\), each root has a corresponding conjugate \\(1-\\sqrt{2}, \\; 2-\\sqrt{3}, \\; 3-\\sqrt{4},\\; \\dots, \\;1000-\\sqrt{1001}\\). Therefore, the polynomial must also have the roots \\(1-\\sqrt{2}, \\; 2-\\sqrt{3}, \\; 3-\\sqrt{4},\\; \\dots, \\;1000-\\sqrt{1001}\\).\n\nSince there are 1000 such pairs \\((1+\\sqrt{2}, 1-\\sqrt{2})\\), \\((2+\\sqrt{3}, 2-\\sqrt{3})\\), \\((3+\\sqrt{4}, 3-\\sqrt{4})\\), ..., \\((1000+\\sqrt{1001}, 1000-\\sqrt{1001})\\), the polynomial must have a degree equal to the number of these pairs, which is 1000.\n\nThus, the smallest possible degree of such a polynomial is \\(\\boxed{1000}\\).",
