      "text": "To find the smallest possible degree of a polynomial with rational coefficients that has all the numbers \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, \\; 2+\\sqrt{3}, \\; 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\; 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) as roots, we need to recognize that for a polynomial with rational coefficients, the conjugate of any irrational root must also be a root of the polynomial. Therefore, the polynomial must have the roots \\(1-\\sqrt{2}, \\; 2-\\sqrt{3}, \\; 3-\\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\; 1000-\\sqrt{1001}\\).\n\nSince each pair of roots \\(1+\\sqrt{2}\\) and \\(1-\\sqrt{2}\\), \\(2+\\sqrt{3}\\) and \\(2-\\sqrt{3}\\), ..., \\(1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) and \\(1000-\\sqrt{1001}\\) contributes two roots to the polynomial, the total number of roots is \\(2 \\times 1000 = 2000\\).\n\nThis means the smallest possible degree of the polynomial is 2000. We can confirm this by constructing the polynomial with these roots. The polynomial can be written as the product of the quadratic polynomials corresponding to each pair of roots:\n\n\\[\n(x - (1+\\sqrt{2}))(x - (1-\\sqrt{2})) = (x-1-\\sqrt{2})(x-1+\\sqrt{2}) = (x-1)^2 - (\\sqrt{2})^2 = x^2 - 2x + 1 - 2 = x^2 - 2x - 1,\n\\]\n\\[\n(x - (2+\\sqrt{3}))(x - (2-\\sqrt{3})) = (x-2-\\sqrt{3})(x-2+\\sqrt{3}) = (x-2)^2 - (\\sqrt{3})^2 = x^2 - 4x + 4 - 3 = x^2 - 4x + 1,\n\\]\nand so on, up to\n\\[\n(x - (1000+\\sqrt{1001}))(x - (1000-\\sqrt{1001})) = (x-1000-\\sqrt{1001})(x-1000+\\sqrt{1001}) = (x-1000)^2 - (\\sqrt{1001})^2 = x^2 - 2000x + 1000^2 - 1001 = x^2 - 2000x + 999^2 - 1 = x^2 - 2000x + 998001.\n\\]\n\nThe polynomial with roots \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, \\; 2+\\sqrt{3}, \\; 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\; 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) is then:\n\\[\n(x - (1+\\sqrt{2}))(x - (2+\\sqrt{3}))(x - (3+\\sqrt{4})) \\cdots (x - (1000+\\sqrt{1001})) = (x^2 - 2x - 1)(x^2 - 4x + 1) \\cdots (x^2 - 2000x + 998001).\n\\]\n\nThe degree of this polynomial is the sum of the degrees of the individual quadratic polynomials, which is \\(2 \\times 1000 = 2000\\).\n\nThus, the smallest possible degree of the polynomial is \\(\\boxed{2000}\\).",
